  <title | Megaherbivores as Ecosystem Engineers>
  <subtitle | Evidence from Sub-Saharan Savanna Systems>
  <author #goodall affiliation="Gombe Institute" orcid=0000-0002-1825-0097 email=jg@gombe.example +corresponding | Jane Goodall>
  <author affiliation="Royal Society" | David Attenborough>
  <date | 2026-05-01>
  <doi | 10.1234/megaherbivores.2026>
  <keywords | elephants, savanna, carbon, ecosystem engineering>
  <abstract | We present evidence that elephant populations shape regional climate by remodelling woody vegetation and soil carbon storage.>
</meta>

<!-- cell: section/form:pipe -->
<!-- cell: section/kwarg:sec-type -->
<section #intro sec-type=intro | Introduction>
Elephant populations have been **declining** for decades, and their role as
ecosystem engineers is *understated* in most climate models. We revisit the
question with three decades of census data.<note | "Megaherbivore" follows Owen-Smith's 1988 usage: body mass above 1000 kg.>

A landmark survey <cite @goodall1971> first quantified the link between browsing
pressure and canopy turnover; see <ref @fig:elephant> and <ref @tab:density>.

<!-- cell: section/kwarg:sec-type -->
<section #methods sec-type=methods | Methods>

<!-- cell: sub-section/form:pipe -->
<sub-section | Data sources>
We compiled census data from twelve national parks.

<!-- cell: sub-sub-section/form:pipe -->
<sub-sub-section | Satellite imagery>
Woody cover came from MODIS at 250 m resolution.

<section #results sec-type=results | Results>
The relationship between density $d$ and turnover is summarised by the model

<!-- cell: display-math/register:sigil -->
<$$ #eq:turnover | T = \alpha d + \beta \log(r) $$>

A robust positive correlation holds across all parks (see <ref @eq:turnover>).

<!-- cell: fig/form:pipe -->
<figure #fig:elephant src="elephant.jpg" | An adult African elephant in Tanzania.>

